Operator-RHS expected-type inference for generic calls
======================================================

When a generic function `f[T](x: T, g: (T,T)->T)` is called as
`f(7, _ + _)`, the placeholder closure `_ + _` is analyzed
*after* its preceding arg has bound `T = int`. The analyzer
must thread `T = int` into the closure's expected type
*before* placeholder inference fires; otherwise the closure
sees `T` still free and the call fails with `cannot infer
type for closure parameter '_phN'`.

Three commits closed this gap on three surfaces (explicit
type-args, operator-dispatch RHS, bare generic calls).
Sysl@eb3fa5673 was the last piece. This file pins all three
shapes so they cannot quietly regress on any backend.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

Bare generic call — placeholder closure as second arg
-----------------------------------------------------

The bare call shape `apply2(7, _ + _)` needs `T` bound from
the first arg's value `7` before `_ + _` is analyzed. With
the fix, the placeholder both knows it's `(int,int) -> int`.

    apply2[T](x: T, f: (T, T) -> T) -> T = f(x, x)

    #test
    test_bare_call_placeholder_rhs() -> unit
        val r = apply2(7, _ + _)
        assert(r == 14, s"7 + 7 should be 14, got $r")

Explicit type-args still work
-----------------------------

Providing `[int]` was always the manual workaround; it
must continue working unchanged.

    #test
    test_explicit_type_args() -> unit
        val r = apply2[int](5, _ * _)
        assert(r == 25, s"5 * 5 should be 25, got $r")

Two-arity inference — both args pin `T` consistently
----------------------------------------------------

    pair_apply[T](a: T, b: T, f: (T, T) -> T) -> T = f(a, b)

    #test
    test_two_arg_inference() -> unit
        val r = pair_apply(3, 4, _ + _)
        assert(r == 7, s"3 + 4 should be 7, got $r")

Closure literal in the slot (not a placeholder)
-----------------------------------------------

A full closure literal `(a, b) -> a - b` also needs its
parameter types pinned by inference from the prior arg.

    #test
    test_closure_literal_rhs() -> unit
        val r = pair_apply(10, 3, (a, b) -> a - b)
        assert(r == 7, s"10 - 3 should be 7, got $r")
